WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.997
Second Normal Form becomes especially relevant when a table has a composite key.

00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:09.977
Suppose the row is identified by appointment ID and service code together.

00:00:09.977 --> 00:00:15.052
Now ask whether every non-key column depends on that whole combination.

00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:18.552
Appointment start time depends only on the appointment.

00:00:18.552 --> 00:00:22.727
Service name and standard price depend only on the service code.

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:28.402
Quantity and the captured charge may depend on the combination of appointment and service.

00:00:28.402 --> 00:00:35.232
The columns that depend on only part of the composite key should be moved to the table where

00:00:35.232 --> 00:00:40.822
that dependency belongs. This leads toward separate appointment, service, and appointment-service tables.

00:00:40.822 --> 00:00:47.897
The goal is to align each fact with the key that actually determines it.

00:00:47.897 --> 00:00:52.732
Connect this idea to an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines.

00:00:52.732 --> 00:00:58.312
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:58.312 --> 00:01:05.827
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:10.887
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:10.887 --> 00:01:16.560
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
